// WIT contract for OTAP dataflow WASM plugins.
//
// UNSTABLE / EXPERIMENTAL: This contract is under active development and is
// NOT stable. Every identifier, signature, and shape below is subject to
// breaking changes without notice while the WASM binary plugin system is being
// built out. Do not depend on this interface for anything outside the
// in-tree experimental slice.
//
// This contract freezes only the tiny surface this vertical slice needs. It
// includes a minimal `lifecycle` export and capability-limited `host-services`
// import (config, logging, and a bounded counter). OPL execution, batch
// building, exporter/receiver/extension worlds, the complete kernel
// vocabulary, and escape hatches are intentionally deferred and will be
// added kernel-by-kernel behind working code.
//
// NOTE: `resource` and `record` are WIT keywords, so the `attr-scope` cases are
// escaped with `%`.

package otel:otap-dataflow-plugin@0.1.0;

/// Host-provided, OTel-semantic operations over host-managed pdata resources.
///
/// The guest only ever holds an opaque `pdata` resource handle; bulk Arrow
/// data never crosses the WASM boundary. Kernels run natively on the host's
/// Arrow arrays.
interface otel-kernels {
    /// Opaque handle to a host-managed pdata resource. The guest cannot inspect
    /// underlying buffers; it can only pass the handle back to host kernels.
    resource pdata;

    /// OTel attribute scope selector.
    enum attr-scope {
        %resource,
        scope,
        %record,
    }

    /// Number of rows in the pdata resource's root record batch.
    pdata-num-rows: async func(data: borrow<pdata>) -> u32;

    /// Return a new pdata resource keeping only rows whose attribute `key` in
    /// `scope` equals `value`. Consumes the input resource and returns a new
    /// resource.
    ///
    /// Current experimental behavior:
    /// - only `record` scope over the root record batch is implemented
    /// - `resource` and `scope` are rejected (trap) until implemented
    /// - invalid keys and invalid data-shape operations are rejected (trap)
    ///
    /// A trap is not recoverable for the plugin instance: it fails the
    /// current `process` call, which the engine treats as a node processing
    /// error and uses to terminate the node. See the `kernel-processor`
    /// world doc comment below.
    filter-by-attribute-eq: async func(
        data: pdata,
        scope: attr-scope,
        key: string,
        value: string,
    ) -> pdata;
}

/// Processor plugin export.
interface processor {
    use otel-kernels.{pdata};

    /// Process one pdata resource. Return `none` to drop the pdata entirely.
    process: async func(data: pdata) -> option<pdata>;
}

/// Host-provided services available to every plugin world that imports this
/// interface. This is deliberately the only project-owned capability surface
/// beyond `otel-kernels`.
///
/// A Rust `std` guest also imports `wasi:cli@0.3.0` and
/// `wasi:clocks@0.3.0`. The host links only those adapter-provided WASI
/// interfaces: CLI state has empty arguments and environment, no initial
/// working directory, and empty stdio; clocks currently expose real wall and
/// monotonic time. Future clock waits fail immediately in every guest entry
/// point. Filesystem, sockets, random, network access, and every other ambient
/// WASI capability remain unavailable.
///
/// Every project-owned operation below is capability-limited by design:
///
/// - `host-abi-version` is a constant, so a guest can detect the host it is
///   talking to before using anything else here.
/// - `get-config` is read-only; there is no way for a guest to write
///   configuration back to the host.
/// - `log` never gives the guest access to the host's raw logging/tracing
///   subsystem; every call is routed through the host's own `otel_*`
///   telemetry macros under the `wasm_processor` component scope, and the
///   host stamps each record with the emitting pipeline node name. Guest logs
///   therefore travel the same routing path as native processor logs while
///   remaining attributable to the specific plugin instance that emitted
///   them; a guest cannot forge or suppress that attribution.
/// - `counter-add` is a single, minimal metric primitive (no registration,
///   no arbitrary metric kinds) and is bounded: the host enforces a small,
///   fixed cap on the number of distinct counter names a single plugin
///   instance may create (see `counter-add` doc comment for the exact bound),
///   so a misbehaving or malicious guest cannot create unbounded telemetry
///   cardinality.
/// - `get-config`, `log`, and `counter-add` share a token-bucket rate limiter
///   (see `log` doc comment): since plugin instances are long-lived
///   telemetry-agent processes rather than short batch jobs, the limiter
///   bounds a guest's *sustained* host-service call rate while still
///   tolerating short bursts, independent of how much Wasmtime fuel a
///   guest's calling loop consumes.
/// - String-copy budgets are independent of call-rate throttling. Canonical
///   lifting rejects more than 16 KiB of encoded string data per lift before
///   copying it. Imported strings (including kernel arguments) and config
///   clones share a 2 MiB UTF-8 byte allowance per guest entry and a lifetime
///   byte bucket of 1 MiB/second with a 2 MiB burst. Incoming arguments are
///   charged after lifting, even for ignored calls; the final failing lift
///   can therefore add up to 32 KiB of decoded strings. Config is charged
///   before cloning. Exhaustion traps, preventing further copy work. These
///   limits were introduced in host-services ABI version 2.
///
/// Stability: `log` (no structured key/value fields) and `counter-add` (no
/// unit, no attributes) are the two shapes here most likely to need a
/// breaking change as this contract grows. Treat them as provisional and
/// gate on `host-abi-version` rather than assuming they are settled.
interface host-services {
    /// Severity level for a guest-emitted log record. Maps 1:1 to the host's
    /// `otel_trace!`/`otel_debug!`/`otel_info!`/`otel_warn!`/`otel_error!`
    /// telemetry macros.
    enum log-level {
        trace,
        debug,
        info,
        warn,
        error,
    }

    /// Return this host's `host-services` ABI version.
    ///
    /// Bumped whenever the observable behavior of an existing operation in
    /// this interface changes. A guest that depends on specific semantics
    /// should check this from `initialize` and return an `init-error` if it
    /// does not recognize the value, so an incompatible pairing fails at
    /// pipeline startup rather than misbehaving at runtime. (Adding a *new*
    /// operation is a world-level change, which an older guest simply does
    /// not import; it is a newer guest against an older host that this
    /// guards against.)
    ///
    /// Not rate-limited: it takes no guest input, allocates nothing, and
    /// returns a constant.
    host-abi-version: async func() -> u32;

    /// Return the plugin-specific configuration blob (JSON, serialized to a
    /// string) for the calling plugin instance.
    ///
    /// This is exactly the `config` value from the host's node configuration
    /// -- the freeform, plugin-owned sub-object -- and nothing else. Host
    /// -internal fields such as the `.wasm` file's path on the host
    /// filesystem are deliberately *not* included: a guest in this world has
    /// no filesystem capability, so it has no use for them, and excluding
    /// them keeps host configuration internals out of the plugin ABI. When
    /// no plugin-specific config is set, the string is `null`.
    ///
    /// This is a read-only, per-instance snapshot fixed at instantiation
    /// time: the same string is returned on every call for the lifetime of
    /// the instance, and there is no host-services operation that lets a
    /// guest mutate it.
    ///
    /// Bound: shares the token bucket described under `log`, because each
    /// call copies the blob into a fresh host allocation. A throttled call
    /// returns an empty string. The copy-byte budget is checked before
    /// cloning and traps on exhaustion, so guests should call this once,
    /// from `initialize`, and keep the result.
    get-config: async func() -> string;

    /// Emit a structured log record through the host's own telemetry
    /// pipeline (never a raw `println!`/stdout write).
    ///
    /// Bound: the host forwards at most 4096 bytes of `message` to telemetry;
    /// longer messages are truncated (not dropped) to a valid UTF-8 prefix
    /// with a truncation marker appended.
    ///
    /// Transient argument copies are bounded separately by the lifting and
    /// aggregate byte budgets described on this interface. Those checks
    /// apply even when call-rate throttling would otherwise ignore the log.
    ///
    /// `get-config`, `log`, and `counter-add` share a token-bucket rate
    /// limiter scoped to the plugin instance's whole lifetime (not reset per
    /// call): it allows a sustained call rate plus a burst allowance, so a
    /// guest calling any of them in a tight loop is throttled down to the
    /// sustained rate rather than either being trapped or generating
    /// unbounded telemetry volume. Calls made while the bucket is empty are
    /// silent no-ops provided the copy-byte budget is not exceeded.
    /// Throttling is visible to operators through the host's
    /// `guest_host_service_calls_rejected` metric, so a plugin that has gone
    /// quiet can be told apart from one that is being throttled.
    log: async func(level: log-level, message: string);

    /// Add `value` to a host-tracked, named counter scoped to this plugin
    /// instance.
    ///
    /// Bound: the host accepts at most 16 distinct counter `name`s per plugin
    /// instance (first-seen-wins), and retains no name longer than 256 bytes.
    /// Calls that exceed either bound are silently no-ops provided the
    /// lifting and copy-byte budgets are not exceeded. This bounds
    /// telemetry cardinality and retained host memory. The two rejection
    /// reasons are reported as separate host metrics
    /// (`guest_counter_add_rejected_name_len` and
    /// `guest_counter_add_rejected_cardinality`), so a silently-dropped call
    /// is still diagnosable. `counter-add` also shares the `log` rate limiter
    /// described above.
    ///
    /// Names within the bound accumulate across calls for the lifetime of the
    /// instance. The host emits the per-name cumulative totals as structured
    /// telemetry on its own metrics-collection tick -- deliberately not once
    /// per call, so a plugin that counts once per batch does not put a log
    /// record on the pdata hot path.
    counter-add: async func(name: string, value: u64);
}

/// Plugin initialization, exported by every plugin so the host can drive
/// one-time setup before the (possibly many) `process` calls made against a
/// single instantiated guest.
interface lifecycle {
    /// Structured error returned when a plugin fails to initialize (for
    /// example, because its configuration -- fetched via
    /// `host-services.get-config` -- is invalid or incomplete). The host
    /// surfaces this as a pipeline *startup*-time configuration error, not a
    /// runtime trap, so misconfigured plugins fail fast before any `process`
    /// call is ever attempted.
    record init-error {
        /// Human-readable description of why initialization failed.
        message: string,
    }

    /// Called exactly once per instantiated guest, immediately after
    /// instantiation and before any `process` call.
    ///
    /// Bound: like `process`, this runs under a Wasmtime fuel budget and a
    /// host-owned deadline. These bounds prevent a guest from hanging pipeline
    /// startup in a loop or an indefinite component-model wait; exceeding
    /// either is reported as a startup configuration error.
    ///
    /// Design note: configuration is intentionally *not* passed as a
    /// parameter here. Instead, the guest calls `host-services.get-config`
    /// from within its own `initialize` implementation. This keeps
    /// `host-services` the single, auditable surface through which
    /// host-provided data reaches the guest, rather than splitting
    /// configuration delivery across two different mechanisms.
    ///
    /// Although this component-model function is async, the processor factory
    /// drives it synchronously so failures remain pipeline construction
    /// errors. Guests must not perform future clock waits here; the host
    /// rejects them immediately. The host-owned deadline also applies when a
    /// guest waits on a component-model waitable set.
    initialize: async func() -> result<_, init-error>;
}

/// Capability tier for kernel-only processor plugins.
///
/// The project-owned capabilities available to a `kernel-processor` guest are
/// `otel-kernels` (host-managed OTel data operations) and `host-services`
/// (version, read-only config, logging, and a bounded counter). Rust `std`
/// guests additionally use the narrowly linked WASI CLI and clocks interfaces
/// described above. No `wasi:http`, `wasi:sockets`, filesystem, random, or
/// other ambient capability is linked. Real clocks are the only intentional
/// source of guest-observable nondeterminism in this capability-constrained
/// compute sandbox.
///
/// Resource bounds the host applies to every instance of this world:
/// - a Wasmtime fuel budget per `initialize`/`process` call, which bounds guest
///   Wasm instructions;
/// - a host-owned wall-clock deadline per `initialize`/`process` call, which
///   also bounds time spent suspended on component-model waitables;
/// - a linear-memory cap on guest allocation;
/// - a 16 KiB encoded-input budget per canonical lift, including exported
///   results, and the aggregate string-copy byte budgets described above;
/// - a cap on host-managed resources during each call, plus a post-`process`
///   requirement that no host resource remains after the returned `pdata` is
///   reclaimed, so complete host-side batches cannot accumulate across calls;
/// - a provisional per-entry cap on native `otel-kernels` invocations,
///   including `pdata-num-rows`, limiting the number of requested operations;
/// - the shared `host-services` token bucket described above.
///
/// These bounds do not guarantee processing latency: a single synchronous
/// native kernel can process a large host-side batch. Guest memory limits do
/// not bound that batch, and fuel/epochs cannot preempt native host execution.
/// Data-size/work budgets and cooperative kernels remain follow-on work.
///
/// Trap semantics: a trap -- from exhausting the fuel or kernel-call budget,
/// from a guest panic, or from violating a kernel contract -- is terminal for
/// the instance. Wasmtime marks the whole instance unusable, and the host does
/// not re-instantiate: the failing `process` call returns an error, which the
/// engine treats as a node processing error and uses to terminate the node.
/// Plugins should therefore treat traps as fatal, not as a per-batch error
/// channel; return `none` from `process` to drop data, and an `init-error`
/// from `initialize` to reject configuration.
///
/// `process` is directly awaited by the engine. Each store permits only one
/// in-flight guest invocation, and future clock waits are rejected so a guest
/// cannot suspend that invocation indefinitely.
world kernel-processor {
    import otel-kernels;
    import host-services;
    export processor;
    export lifecycle;
}
